### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim that the umbilical cord must not be cut until one hour after birth is refuted by medical evidence and guidelines. Major health organizations, including the [American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG)](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/experts-and-stories/ask-acog/what-is-delayed-cord-clamping), recommend delayed cord clamping for only 30 to 60 seconds after birth, and the World Health Organization suggests a delay of at least 1-3 minutes. Research indicates that the transfer of beneficial blood from the placenta to the newborn [typically plateaus around 140 seconds (about 2.5 minutes) after birth](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4687772/), making a one-hour delay physiologically unnecessary. The assertion that a baby will "not have enough blood" without waiting an hour is an oversimplification, as risks are associated with very early clamping, not the standard recommended delays.